  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 213 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A retrograding area of low pressure system near Nova \nScotia will bring some isolated to scattered rain showers to the \nregion throughout the day. Models are showing weak moisture \ntransport but steepening mid-level lapse rates from cold advection \naloft with be able to utilize the modest moisture and produce some \nshowers through the day. We don't expect any heavy rainfall with \namounts likely peaking around a tenth of an inch. Most places, \nhowever, will remain dry through the day as areal coverage will not \nbe overly impressive. Rain chances will diminish as we head into \nSunday morning but an isolated shower cannot be ruled out for the \nfirst part of Sunday. Thereafter, deep layer ridging will become \nestablished across the North Country and northern New York with \nmostly clear skies, light winds, and seasonably cool temperatures \nexpected on both Monday and Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Towards the middle of next week, a deepening low \ncentered well to our north will move eastward from Ontario to \nQuebec, with associated frontal boundaries bringing more widespread \nprecipitation to our region, with the most widespread showers \ncurrently expected Wednesday night into Thursday. Although there \nwill be plenty of moisture, the better dynamics will be further \nnorth and the nocturnal timing will limit instability so widespread \nthunderstorm activity is not expected but trends will need to be \nmonitored as we get closer. Behind this system, cooler weather will \nreturn for the end of the week, with high temperatures on Friday \ngenerally in the 60s to near 70 across the region.",
